# `@jesscss/internal-css-recognition`

- **Role**: the **shared CSS recognition surface**. All four AST grammars
  (`css`/`less`/`scss`/`jess` `src/ast/grammar.ts`) `composeLeaf` over it. It is the one
  place a CSS construct can be added so that every dialect gets it.
- **Exports**:
  - `recognition.ts` → `cssAstSyntax`, `lessAstSyntax`
  - `pseudo-consts.ts` → `cssAstPseudoSyntax`
  - `opaque-at-rule.ts` → `opaqueAtRuleRecognition`
- **Build**: `pnpm --filter @jesscss/internal-css-recognition build`

## ⛔ Build it FIRST

All four parsers link against this package's `lib/`. Building a parser against a **stale**
`lib/` links it to an old recognition surface and masks failures — the standard trap in a
fresh worktree. Order: **internal-css-recognition → parsers → …**

## If you are here to add a rule

Adding a CSS construct here serves all four dialects. That is the point of the package, and
it is under-used: the four AST grammars currently each restate the whole of CSS with
disjoint rule families. Prefer adding here over adding to a dialect.

Conversely, do **not** put a dialect-specific construct here. A Less mixin call, a Jess
collection and a Sass placeholder are genuinely their own things and belong in their own
grammars.

## Constraints

- Recognition only — pre-final `composeLeaf` pieces must prove recognition-only, so rules
  here carry no semantic builders.
- Contains **zero** `node()` boundaries today. A CST host can only emit a node where the
  grammar calls `node()`, so this surface currently projects no structure.
- Macro-compiled: `pnpm run check:macro` must stay at **0 interpreter fallbacks**. A
  fallback build emits a *different tree*.
- No regex outside `regex()`; no literal non-ASCII in a regex.

See `.cursor/rules/domains/parsers.mdc` for the full grammar-writing rules and the two-route
(AST vs CST) model.
